Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:08.310 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  myanmar is a country in free form a coup  in february derailed democracy sparking  mass uprising and a brutal crackdown by  the authorities  security forces are accused of murder of  sexual and physical assault  of torture with our team in myanmar this  is our investigation into abuse in  [Music]  meet custody ton  the engineering student dreaming of  going to japan  the clever son  the adored friend  just 21 years old  in february he was at college in central  myanmar  then the military staged a coup  three months later  cinco ton  is dead  as protests against the general's power  grab surge  zinko tun joins rallies in mandalay  posting his defiance on social media  this is the start of his resistance  a resistance that led to arrest  and death  he couldn't accept the detectorship and  give his future to the gentile  he was in the first line of defense ko  yanwin knew zimko tun well  as with all the witnesses in myanmar in  this report we've changed his name and  voice for protection  by may zinko tun had joined an armed  group of volunteers fighting junta  forces  during one battle  he's shot  captured and taken away by the  authorities  this is the last known picture of him  alive  holding his injured right arm  he was arrested as an insurgent  so the military will torture him  definitely  one is lucky they're only beaten in  there and released with some wounds  but most are injured for life after  being arrested and some die  news of the fighting in zinkotan's  village is on military tv the night of  his arrest  the six captured alleged terrorists are  the same group pictured under the tree  but only five are in these photos  zinkotan  is missing  the next day zinkotan's family is called  to collect his body  ko yanwin saw his corpse  there were wounds on the body which are  the signs of being tortured  when i found the injury on the back of  his head the area was really soft  i found some injuries around his abdomen  and lips  his bhagata the federation of twin  union's tattoo had also been removed  from all of the people that we've spoken  to who knew zinkotan and also from the  photos of his body that i've managed to  see there's a definite suggestion that  something violent has happened and i  mean if i look at this photo there's  string around his wrist why is it there  but the problem of course is that i'm  not an expert  and that's why we decided to contact the  professionals  we contacted physicians for human rights  who asked a forensic pathologist to look  at our evidence  dr karen kelly gave us a detailed report  he was a previously healthy 21 year old  the gunshot injury appeared not to be  life-threatening  because there are photos of him with it  bandaged and alive  and  so to me this would indicate that there  was some element of homicide  to this death  that would be my number one concern is  that that he was actually a homicide  death  how confident are you in your belief  that this was  a homicide  oh very confident and those most likely  responsible for the death are  those who arrested and held him captive  that would be my opinion yesterday  [Music]  isn't the only one to allegedly die in  the custody of myanmar's security forces  like him  others were dead after just a few hours  this is poet ket t  he died less than a day after his arrest  his family say he was tortured  then there's opposition politician kin  mung lat  he died in custody after just one night  friends say his body was covered in  injuries  officials claim he fainted  finally activist and teacher saw me at  lynn  his wife was called to collect his body  from a military hospital  state media claimed he fell on a steel  pipe trying to evade arrest  these are just four of the suspicious  deaths that we know about  campaigners believe there are many more  so we find that the  the military junta is using a systematic  campaign to terrorize its own people and  attack its own people and that includes  arrest and detention and beatings in  custody  this is a man  famous poet  and he  was detained along with his wife  and held in custody for one night  and his body was returned to the family  the next day and was there any  reason why he might have died in custody  did he have any health problems  his relatives said he was in good health  and he had no health problems at all the  next day he was returned to his family  just his body and he showed signs of  torture  so you think this is a deliberate  strategy to  spread fear essentially undermine the  enemy  the janta is trying to create an  environment of fear and intimidation so  people will not speak out against the  military junta  more than a thousand people have been  killed by junta forces since february  many shot at protests  some  even gunned down in the street  thousands more have been arrested  we were forced to kneel down with our  hands on our heads in the street  they were pointing guns in my face when  they found us and i was also beaten as i  was arrested  by the end of march protests had been  happening daily  schwermann was at a sit-in when security  forces arrested her  she was then taken to one of the dreaded  interrogation centers for questioning  police officers brutally physically  violated both women and men  in the interrogation center the male  detainees building was just next to us  we were always hearing those screams and  tortured sounds  and i also witnessed one woman who was  the same age as me  there were bruises all over her swollen  face and bleeding wounds on her mouth  she took off her blouse to show bruises  on every inch on her body  many of the former inmates say they were  detained at protests  others taken from their homes at night  and then the interrogation begins  i talked with one young woman  she said she was beaten up so badly by  the soldiers when she got arrested  both the soldiers and police continued  to abuse her at the police station  like brutally kicking at the woman's  private parts  they used a police baton and a metal  broomstick to beat her up  the police even sat on her back while  torturing her  aemia was arrested in yangon  after being questioned by police she was  driven to the northern edge of the city  along this road to schwapita  interrogation center  a place synonymous with fear  it's too dangerous for our crews to film  at the site at the moment but this is  what we've managed to piece together  from former detainees there are four  main buildings all of them single story  kind of like hearts there's one for men  one for women  one for staff  and then there's this one the secluded  one  this is the one where they say people  were taken handcuffed and blindfolded  this is the one  where they say people are beaten and  tortured  all of us were brutally violated both  physically and mentally especially  the boys who were brought to the  secluded building  i didn't see anything but we could hear  the bawling and screaming  beatings by the authorities are  frequently reported  some are done in plain sight on the  streets but the women we spoke to  described systematic abuse by the  security forces behind closed doors  there was a woman  she looked a mess and there were so many  wounds  her jaw was broken also her nose and  cheekbones  and one of her eyes is black but she  could see  there were bruises everywhere especially  on her buttocks  this is the woman amir is describing 31  year old kenya ii  a dancer who trained in the uk  she was arrested at home hours after a  series of bombs had exploded at a nearby  local government office the next day  military run tv aired details of her  arrest  accusing her of being a bomb maker  but look carefully  something is very wrong  this is king a2 less than 24 hours after  her detention  you can see her face was  almost unrecognizable  the amount of swelling  and bruising  are consistent with significant blow to  her face and how did you and the family  feel when you saw that photograph what  did you say to each other when you saw  it  very upset very very very upset and very  worried about her  we were worried that her life  could be in danger  stop killing protesters  now tantz has been campaigning in  manchester for kenya's release  for almost three weeks her family heard  nothing while she was in the  interrogation center  then she was allowed to make one phone  call  she simply said to her mom that not to  worry about her  she is okay  in my opinion it is unlikely that she  was able to speak freely  in june state media broadcast an edited  confession where king yay two seemingly  admits to a range of crimes including  making weapons and arson her godmother  says she's surprised by the confession  and fears it may have been coerced  kenya two is now being held in the  notorious insane prison with thousands  of others  allegations of abuse continue to emerge  this is one of the largest attacks that  the junta has led against its own people  we're talking about thousands of people  being arrested hundreds of people being  killed is anyone safe  no i don't think anyone is safe in  myanmar if you're against the military  janta if you're calling for democracy  human rights in myanmar there's a  well-founded fear of arrest detention  torture in custody and even being killed  in the streets while protesting  killing torture  abuse  the military regime hasn't responded to  any of the allegations we put to them  questions over deaths and violence  continue to go unanswered  in a few short months myanmar has gone  from a fledgling democracy to a country  in crisis  since february's coup throughout our  reporting we and others have uncovered  claims of abuse and killing in custody  by police and military  those meant to protect the people  now stand accused of terrorizing them

